```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.path = "figures/panel-", fig.width = 7, fig.height = 4.5)
```

This vignette follows a crime panel from the published archive to a table you
can estimate on, and shows what the package refuses to do quietly along the
way.

```{r library}
library(streetlamp)
```

## The archive is a stack of snapshots, not a database

data.police.uk publishes one zip per month. Each holds every force's street,
outcomes and stop-and-search files for a window of months, so the same
force-month appears in many snapshots and its contents can differ between
them. `streetlamp` never merges those versions: it lists them, picks one by an
explicit rule, and records the alternatives.

The package ships two miniature snapshots so that this runs offline.

```{r register}
cache <- tempfile("streetlamp-cache-")
zips <- list.files(
  system.file("extdata", "archive", package = "streetlamp"),
  pattern = "zip$", full.names = TRUE
)
for (z in zips) lamp_archive_register(z, dir = cache)

snap <- lamp_archive_snapshot(cache)
snap
```

Two snapshots cover the same months, so most files exist twice.

```{r versions}
versions <- lamp_list_versions(snap)
versions[, c("force_id", "month", "file_type", "n_versions", "differs")]
```

`differs` compares checksums. Street files almost always differ between
snapshots, because outcomes attached to old crimes keep being updated, so a
checksum change does not mean the force re-supplied the month. To see what
actually changed, compare the versions:

```{r diff}
lamp_version_diff(snap, "west-yorkshire", "2026-05", "outcomes")
```

Ten crime identifiers present in the June snapshot are gone from the July one.
That is a real revision, and it is why the version used is recorded in the
contract of everything built from it.

## Reading

The readers return a fixed schema with the raw labels preserved.

```{r read}
crime <- lamp_read_crime(snap)
crime[1:3, c("crime_id", "month", "force_id", "crime_type", "lsoa_code", "is_asb")]
```

Two things are worth pausing on.

```{r asb}
asb <- crime[crime$is_asb, ]
c(asb_rows = nrow(asb), with_a_crime_id = sum(!is.na(asb$crime_id)))
```

Anti-social behaviour carries no crime identifier and no outcome, because it
is not a crime. It stays in its own series and out of the crime total.

```{r vintage}
lamp_lsoa_vintage(crime)[, c("force_id", "month", "vintage", "basis", "n_only_2021")]
```

The publisher moved from 2011 to 2021 LSOA boundaries with the June 2023 data,
but archive snapshots keep each month at the vintage it was first published
with. The reader therefore detects the vintage of every file separately, by
matching codes against the bundled ONS lists.

## Building the panel

`lamp_panel()` turns records into a balanced area-by-month table. The panel
covers every area in the police force areas of the forces present, not only
the areas that happen to have a recorded crime.

```{r panel}
panel <- lamp_panel(crime, area = "lad")
panel[1:4, c("area", "month", "force_id", "crime_total", "asb", "coverage_status")]
```

Districts with no recorded crime in a submitted month are zeros. Districts in
a month the force did not submit are `NA`. That distinction is the point:

```{r missing}
wider <- lamp_read_crime(snap, months = c("2026-04", "2026-05"), forces = c("dyfed-powys", "west-yorkshire"))
gap <- lamp_panel(wider, area = "lad")
table(gap$coverage_status, is.na(gap$crime_total))
```

April is missing from both forces in these snapshots, so every April cell is
`NA` rather than zero. An estimator that treated those as zero crimes would
read the gap as a collapse in crime.

## The coverage audit

```{r coverage}
stops <- lamp_read_stop_counts(snap, forces = c("west-yorkshire", "dyfed-powys"))
full <- lamp_panel(crime, stops = NULL, area = "pfa")
cov <- lamp_coverage(stops)
cov
```

Dyfed-Powys submitted crime files for these months but no stop-and-search
files. The audit marks that mismatch, because a month with an outcome and no
treatment measurement cannot contribute to an estimate of the treatment's
effect.

```{r compare}
lamp_coverage_compare(
  cov, c("2026-05", "2026-05"), c("2026-06", "2026-07"),
  file_type = "stop-and-search"
)
```

## The contract

Everything built from the archive carries its provenance.

```{r contract}
lamp_contract(panel)
```

The contract travels with the table through subsetting and dplyr verbs, and a
copy is attached to every estimate, so a result can always be traced back to
the snapshot and version it came from.

## The bundled sample

A ready-made panel of both sample forces over two years is included, which the
other vignettes use.

```{r sample}
sample_panel <- lamp_sample_panel()
sample_panel
table(lamp_coverage(sample_panel)$status)
```

```{r plot-panel, fig.alt = "Monthly crime totals by force, with months that were not submitted shaded"}
plot(sample_panel)
```
